      "content": "okay, the cleaning residue idea is definitely something i can bring to the facility management team. that's a much more logical explanation. \n\nyou were saying something about the patterns being \"fuzzy\" or irregular. one of the things that really threw us off is that even when we used specific testing strips to check for pH imbalance, everything seemed fine, yet those tiny, erratic scratches are still appearing in these tight, clustered groups. \n\nit's almost as if the damage is being done by something very small and localized, moving in quick, darting motions rather than the broad strokes of a mop or a footstep. if it's not a chemical residue issue, could it be related to physical contaminants that are being kicked up by a specific type of cleaning equipment or even just the way the air circulation handles dust in the lobby? i need to understand the mechanics of how something could create such a \"chaotic\" looking mark if it isn't a simple surface scratch.",
